<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Popol Vuh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1550s</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Popol Vuh, 'Council Book', is the sacred narrative of the K'iche' Maya of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Written in K'iche' in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet by anonymous nobles in the 1550s, it recounts the creation of the world, the exploits of the Hero Twins Hunahpu and Xbalanque, and the history of the K'iche' lineages. It survives in a copy made by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friar</a> Francisco Ximénez around 1701.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/popol-vuh.jpg" alt="Popol Vuh" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Ximénez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Popol_vuh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Popol Vuh is a foundational sacred narrative of the Kʼicheʼ people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, one of the Maya peoples. Its name is translated as Book of the Community or Book of Counsel, literally the book that pertains to the mat, because a woven mat served as a royal throne in ancient Kʼicheʼ society and symbolised the unity of the community. The story was preserved by oral tradition until about 1550, when it was written down.</p><p>Ximénez is generally thought to have borrowed a phonetic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> from a parishioner, a rendering of an oral recitation made near Santa Cruz del <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiche-people">Quiché</a> soon after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a>'s conquest of 1524. Adrián Recinos and Dennis Tedlock, comparing the genealogy at the end with dated colonial records, suggest a date between 1554 and 1558. The preamble states that it is written under the Law of God and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> because the old Popol Vuh can no longer be seen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The text has no divisions in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, but popular editions follow the arrangement of Charles Étienne Brasseur de Bourbourg in 1861: a preamble and four books. Book One tells of the creation of animals and then humans; the first humans, made of earth and mud, crumbled, and the second, made of wood, were washed away in a flood, and the survivors became monkeys. The Hero Twins Hunahpú and Xbalanqué kill Vucub-Caquix and his sons in Book Two.</p><p>In Book Three their father Hun-Hunahpú and uncle Vucub-Hunahpú are killed at a ballgame in the underworld Xibalba. The twins, conceived after their father's death, defeat the Lords of Xibalba and become the sun and the moon. In Book Four humans are made successfully from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, and the book follows the movement of the Kʼicheʼ. Depictions of the Hero Twins and other episodes appear on Maya ceramics, and a stucco frieze at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-mirador">El Mirador</a> may show them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In 1701 the Dominican priest Francisco Ximénez came to Santo Tomás Chichicastenango and transcribed the work, setting out the Kʼicheʼ and a Spanish translation in parallel columns. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> passed to the University of San Carlos after the clerics were expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> in 1829–30. Carl Scherzer copied part of it in 1854, and in 1855 Charles Étienne Brasseur de Bourbourg took the volume to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It reached the Newberry Library in Chicago through the collection of Edward E. Ayer, where Adrián Recinos rediscovered it in 1941. Translators include Delia Goetz, Munro Edmonson and Dennis Tedlock. In 2012 the text was declared intangible cultural heritage of Guatemala.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Popol Vuh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Far to the south, the <b>Popol Vuh</b> of the K’iche’ Maya traces the ancestors of their lords to a place called Tulan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/toltec-state">Toltec state · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From the early colonial period come the <b>Popol Vuh</b>, the Ritual of the Bacabs and, at least in part, the Chilam Balam books.</blockquote><a href="/religion/maya-religion">Maya religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The K’iche' Maya told in the <b>Popol Vuh</b> how the gods had made people of mud and then of wood before they succeeded, making the first true humans from maize dough.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>…the gods and describe royal rituals, scenes painted on pottery, the almanacs of the surviving books, and the <b>Popol Vuh</b>, written down by K’iche' nobles in the 1550s, which tells of the making of the first people from maize and of the Hero Twins, Hunahpu and Xbalanque, who…</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Popol Vuh</b> was written down in the 1550s, and K'iche' remains Guatemala's most widely spoken Maya language.</blockquote><a href="/state/kiche-kingdom">K'iche' kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
